{7 = 7-19}
35=7.	The Hoe having engaged in a dispute with the Plough, 
39=8.	the Hoe addressed the Plough: @q[Hoe,Plough]{“Plough, 
43=9.	you draw furrows -- what does your furrowing matter to me? 
47=10.	You break clods -- what does your clod-breaking matter to me? 
51=11.	When water overflows you cannot dam it up. 
55=12.	You cannot fill baskets with earth. You cannot press (?) clay to make bricks. 
59=13.	You cannot lay foundations or build a house. 
63=14.	You cannot strengthen an old wall's base. 
67=15.	You cannot put a roof on a good man's house. 
71=16.	Plough, you cannot straighten the town squares. 
75=17.	Plough, 
79=18.	you draw furrows -- what does your furrowing matter to me? 
83=19.	You make clods -- what does your clod-making matter to me?”} 

{20 = 20-33}
87=20.	The Plough addressed the Hoe: @q[Plough,Hoe]{“I am the Plough, 
91=21.	fashioned by great strength, assembled by great hands, 
95=22.	the mighty registrar of Father @wDN{Enlil}. 
99=23.	I am mankind's faithful farmer. 
103=24.	To perform my festival in the fields in the harvest month, 
107=25.	the king slaughters cattle and sacrifices sheep, 
111=26.	and he pours beer into a bowl. 
